---
import { PUBLIC_API_URL } from 'astro:env/server';
import DeveloperPage from '../../components/DeveloperPage.astro';
import { apiOperationCount, apiOrigin, mcpPath, mcpToolGroups, webhookEvents } from '../../lib/developers';
import { pages } from '../../lib/pages';

const api = apiOrigin(PUBLIC_API_URL);
const tools = mcpToolGroups().reduce((count, group) => count + group.tools.length, 0);
const firstCall = `curl ${api}/v1/jobs \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer shk_..."`;
---

<DeveloperPage
  page={pages.developers}
  heading="Build on ScreenHire"
  lead="Three ways in, on every plan: the API for your own systems, webhooks when something happens, and MCP for an AI assistant."
>
  <h2>Three ways in</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The API.</strong> {apiOperationCount()} routes for jobs, applications, candidates, scorecards, templates, reports and, with
      its own scope, verification results. <a href={pages.developerApi.path}>API reference</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Webhooks.</strong> {webhookEvents().length} events, each a signed POST to your endpoint, retried until it is answered.{' '}
      <a href={pages.developerWebhooks.path}>Webhooks</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>MCP.</strong> {tools} tools an AI assistant such as Claude can use to work with your hiring.{' '}
      <a href={pages.developerMcp.path}>MCP for AI assistants</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Make your first call</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In ScreenHire, an Owner or Admin opens Settings, Integrations, API keys and creates a key. Copy it: it is shown once.</li>
    <li>Send it as a bearer token. This lists your jobs:</li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>{firstCall}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The answer is JSON. Every route lives under <code>/v1</code> on your API address, <code>{api}</code>, and the{' '}
    <a href={pages.developerAuthentication.path}>authentication</a> page says what each kind of key may do.
  </p>

  <h2>Connect an AI assistant</h2>
  <p>
    Point any MCP client at <code>{api}{mcpPath}</code>. A client that signs in with OAuth, such as a custom connector, needs nothing
    else: it asks you to sign in to ScreenHire and choose what it may do. Anything it does shows in the candidate's activity
    under its name.
  </p>
</DeveloperPage>
